# MCP Tools: vercel

ALWAYS include `arguments` in CallMcpTool calls. NEVER send empty `{}`.

### add_toolbar_reaction
Add an emoji reaction to a message in a toolbar thread.

| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| `threadId` | string | yes |  | The thread ID containing the message |
| `messageId` | string | yes |  | The message ID to react to |
| `teamId` | string | yes |  | The team ID to get the deployment events for. Alternatively the team slug can be used.<br>Team IDs start with "team_".<br>If you do not know the team ID or slug, it can be found through these mechanism:<br>- Read the file .vercel/project.json if it exists and extract the orgId<br>- Use the `list_teams` tool |
| `emoji` | string | yes |  | The emoji to add as a reaction (e.g. [emoji]) |

Example:
CallMcpTool({ server: "vercel", toolName: "add_toolbar_reaction", arguments: {"threadId": "value", "messageId": "value", "teamId": "team_xxx", "emoji": "value"} })

### change_toolbar_thread_resolve_status
Change the resolve status of a toolbar thread. Can be used to mark a thread as resolved or unresolve a previously resolved thread.

| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| `threadId` | string | yes |  | The thread ID to update |
| `teamId` | string | yes |  | The team ID to get the deployment events for. Alternatively the team slug can be used.<br>Team IDs start with "team_".<br>If you do not know the team ID or slug, it can be found through these mechanism:<br>- Read the file .vercel/project.json if it exists and extract the orgId<br>- Use the `list_teams` tool |
| `resolved` | boolean | yes |  | Set to true to resolve the thread, false to unresolve it |

Example:
CallMcpTool({ server: "vercel", toolName: "change_toolbar_thread_resolve_status", arguments: {"threadId": "value", "teamId": "team_xxx", "resolved": true} })

