### get_deployment_build_logs
Get the build logs for a deployment by ID or URL, to investigate why a build failed. Returns the most recent lines by default (where build errors appear). Use errorsOnly to see just the failing lines.

| Field | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|-------|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| `idOrUrl` | string | yes |  | The unique identifier or hostname of the deployment. |
| `direction` | enum | no | `"tail"` | Which end of the build log to return. "tail" (default) returns the most recent lines, where build errors appear. "head" returns the earliest lines. Allowed: `tail`, `head` |
| `errorsOnly` | boolean | no | `false` | Return only error, stderr, exit, and fatal events. |
| `limit` | number | no | `100` | Maximum number of log lines to return. Defaults is 100. |
| `since` | string | no |  | Start time - ISO date or relative (e.g. "1h", "30m"). |
| `until` | string | no |  | End time - ISO date or relative. |
| `buildId` | string | no |  | Filter to a specific build ID (bld_...) for multi-build deployments. |
| `teamId` | string | yes |  | The team ID to get the deployment events for. Alternatively the team slug can be used.<br>Team IDs start with "team_".<br>If you do not know the team ID or slug, it can be found through these mechanism:<br>- Read the file .vercel/project.json if it exists and extract the orgId<br>- Use the `list_teams` tool |

Example:
CallMcpTool({ server: "vercel", toolName: "get_deployment_build_logs", arguments: {"idOrUrl": "https://example.com", "teamId": "team_xxx"} })

